001111010011100, which corresponds to the integer 43261596. The method sign=
ature lists this as an int (I'm working in python, so there's unlimited pre=
cision for these). The test cases list the input as 00000010100101000001111=
010011100. The actual input is instead 43261596. The problem wants you to c=
onvert from an int to the bit representation, reverse it, then convert back=
. And that would be fine if that's what the problem said to do! But instead=
 it describes being given an incomplete string representation of a 32 bit n=
umber, filling it out with any missing leading zeroes, reversing it, and co=
nverting it to a normal int.</p>
<p>I don't like having my time wasted by imprecise or actively misleading p=
roblem descriptions. Half the difficulty here comes from guessing what the =
writer meant and failed to articulate, not from writing the actual algorith=
